cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If this software contributes to work you publish, please cite it. A citation is the
  thing that helps; co-authorship is not requested and could not be required -- see the
  Citation section of README.md for why, and for the standing invitation to collaborate
  where a joint contribution is genuinely earned. Licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0 at your
  option; the `license` field below names one of the two because this format and Zenodo
  each store exactly one, and `Cargo.toml` carries the authoritative expression.
title: "pantometry: physics for simulated worlds, checked against closed forms"
abstract: >-
  A Rust workspace of sixteen crates: a kernel that knows conservation, integration,
  scheduling and sampling but no physics, ten domain crates built on it, and scene and
  view layers above. Every claim is checked against a closed form, an exact limit, a
  conservation law or a convergence rate rather than against a second implementation.
  Results are bit-for-bit reproducible across platforms, optimisation levels,
  WebAssembly and thread counts, with a pinned digest that says so.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Park
    given-names: Younghyeon
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4733-5049"
# ASCII only, above and below. The one non-ASCII character this file had was an em dash in `message`,
# which is valid YAML and valid UTF-8 -- and Zenodo's only report is `{"errors": "Citation metadata load
# failed"}`, a message that names no field and cannot distinguish a bad licence from a bad byte. Three
# depositions have died at the load step, so every plausible load hazard goes at once rather than one per
# attempt: no BOM, no CRLF, no non-ASCII, and one licence identifier.
#
# ONE identifier, and this line has now failed a deposition twice.
#
# The project is `MIT OR Apache-2.0`. v0.13.0 put that SPDX **expression** here; CFF's schema takes an
# identifier or a list of them and an expression is neither, so Zenodo rejected it. v0.14.0 put the
# **list**, which is valid CFF and validates clean -- and Zenodo rejected that too, because Zenodo stores
# exactly one licence per record and a list is not one. Valid CFF and valid Zenodo are different things,
# and satisfying the schema is not the same as satisfying the consumer.
#
# So: one identifier, Apache-2.0, matching `.zenodo.json` beside this file. The real licensing is
# unchanged and is stated by `Cargo.toml` and the two LICENSE files; `message` below says so in prose,
# because a citation record that names one of two licences should say that is what it is doing.
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/YounghyeonPark/pantometry"
version: 0.16.0
date-released: "2026-08-20"
keywords:
  - physics simulation
  - verification
  - closed-form solutions
  - conservation laws
  - reproducibility
  - Rust
